  <dialog class="coverage-dialog" id="coverageDialog" aria-labelledby="coverageTitle"><button class="close-button" id="closeCoverage" aria-label="Close coverage information">×</button><p class="eyebrow">PUBLISHED TIMETABLE COVERAGE</p><h2 id="coverageTitle">What’s in this map?</h2><p class="coverage-intro">Missing lines do not mean there are no trains. This panel describes our published dataset, not which trains run or where a Eurail pass is valid.</p><p class="coverage-small" id="coverageDates">Dated timetable snapshot</p><p id="coverageSummary" class="coverage-audit-summary"></p><label class="coverage-country-select" for="coverageCountry"><span>Country</span><select id="coverageCountry"><option value="">All countries</option></select></label><div id="coverageBody" aria-live="polite"></div></dialog>
  <dialog class="method-dialog" id="methodDialog" aria-labelledby="methodTitle"><button class="close-button" id="closeMethod" aria-label="Close data information">×</button><p class="eyebrow">A MAP WITH ITS SOURCES ATTACHED</p><h2 id="methodTitle">Read the network.<br><em>Know the limits.</em></h2><div id="methodContent"><section class="about-guide" id="aboutGuide" aria-labelledby="aboutGuideTitle"><h3 class="detail-heading" id="aboutGuideTitle">HOW IT WORKS</h3><p>Euro Rail maps scheduled trains across Europe for one week: <strong data-about-week>the dated service week</strong>. It is built from <span data-about-sources>published timetable sources</span>. Each line joins stations of the same train, and its colour shows how many services run there per day.</p><ol class="about-steps"><li><strong>Click a line</strong> to see the trains on it. Where several station pairs share one stretch of track, you first get a list, busiest first: pick one. Each route shows its train numbers. On a phone, tap the line.</li><li><strong>Click a station</strong> to see every route that calls there. Those routes are highlighted and other lines turn grey. To find one by name, use the search button on the map or press <kbd>/</kbd>; you can search for a city too. On a phone, tap the station dot or use search.</li><li><strong>Pick a train.</strong> With a line or station open, choose a route in the panel. Its full route is highlighted on the map. Then choose a train number, or a departure for its times and stops. With a station open you can also pick on the map: click one of its highlighted lines (<kbd>Shift</kbd>-click works the same) and that route opens in the panel, with the station still selected. Where several of the station’s routes share the stretch you clicked, you get a short list of just those: pick one. A grey line or empty map works as before. On a phone, tap a highlighted line, or tap the same buttons in the panel.</li><li><strong>Go back</strong> with the ← link at the top of the panel; from a route you picked at a station it returns to that station’s routes. Restore map, Clear selection, the × button or <kbd>Esc</kbd> return to the whole map. On a phone the details open as a sheet: tap its handle or Show on map to see the map.</li></ol><div class="whats-new" id="aboutWhatsNew" role="region" aria-labelledby="whatsNewTitle"><h3 class="detail-heading" id="whatsNewTitle">WHAT’S NEW</h3><div class="whats-new-body" id="whatsNewBody" aria-live="polite"><p class="helper whats-new-empty">Loading the latest changes…</p></div><button type="button" class="whats-new-all" id="whatsNewAll">Show all updates</button></div><h3 class="detail-heading" id="aboutPlay">THINGS TO PLAY WITH</h3><ul class="about-things"><li><strong>Frequency</strong> colours every line by services per day.</li><li><strong>Traffic</strong> estimates how many trains use each shared railway section.</li><li><strong>Eurail</strong> shows services a Eurail pass includes, with reservation and supplement filters.</li><li><strong>Hubs</strong> shows direct trains between the top 50 to 500 major cities.</li><li><strong>Countries</strong> frames one country’s network.</li><li><strong>Filters</strong> sets the dates (all week, weekdays, weekend or one day), a minimum frequency, trains or replacement buses, station dots and more. Advanced controls holds sources and geometry.</li><li><strong>City transit → Trams &amp; metros</strong>, in Filters, adds city trams, metros, funiculars and lifts. It is off by default and has its own week.</li><li><strong>Buses ↗</strong> opens a separate map of buses and coaches.</li><li><strong>Explore ↗</strong> shows every train moving through a day, how far you can get from a station, speeds and station rhythms.</li><li><strong>Share view</strong> copies a link to exactly what you see, with your filters and selection.</li><li>In a line or station panel, tap a day in the weekly chart to show just that date.</li></ul></section><section id="timetableObservationWarning" class="timetable-observations" aria-label="Timetable observation notices" hidden></section><section class="about-sources" id="aboutSources"><h3 class="detail-heading">COVERAGE & SOURCES</h3><p>Explore the country coverage and source register for this dated snapshot.</p><button id="coverageButton" class="primary-button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="coverageDialog">Explore coverage & sources ↗</button><p id="swissCredit" hidden>Timetable data: <a href="https://opentransportdata.swiss/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">opentransportdata.swiss</a></p></section><p>This atlas represents scheduled services for <strong data-about-week>the dated service week</strong>. Frequencies count trains and confirmed temporary replacement buses together as services; they do not measure passenger use or prove actual running. Temporary replacements are marked and can be hidden in Filters. Frequency counts services between recorded station calls. Overlapping express and local paths are not summed into a single physical-track total. Selecting a line adds an outline and keeps its frequency color. Where strokes overlap, clicking selects the visible stroke. Frequency includes recorded same-service journeys through intermediate calls. Traffic estimates train traversals on shared railway sections. Hubs follows retained railway paths for direct services between selected major cities, leaving unsupported geometry as gaps and counting each route separately; Countries frames the available network in one country.</p><p>National and regional source feeds can overlap. Source filters retain that distinction; these are not certified counts of unique trains across Europe.</p><p>The Eurail views show canonical included services plus a small, separately labelled set reviewed against current primary sources for Global Pass second class. The optional amber overlay shows conditional and unknown pass eligibility, separately from confirmed included counts. Known exclusions stay excluded. Eurail fee checkboxes can match any selected condition or require all of them, counting each included service once. All and None select every fee category or no fee category; No extras only requires explicit evidence of no mandatory reservation and no listed train supplement. Fees unresolved never qualify as free. Turn off confirmed inclusion and enable uncertain coverage to inspect only unknown or conditional pass eligibility. “No extra charge required” means no mandatory reservation or listed train supplement; optional reservations, upgrades and accommodation may cost extra. Unknown fees never qualify as free. Seat availability is not inferred.</p><p>Railway alignments are source-provided or inferred from physical infrastructure. Recovered paths are solid and have an independent “Inferred rail paths” switch. They follow the shortest connected known railway path available between recorded stops, which may differ from the train’s operating route. They do not certify exact operating track. Known services with unresolved geometry use dashed links between recorded stops. These lines can cut across land or water: they do not trace the tracks used. Click a line for an explanation, or uncheck “Approximate paths · dotted” in Filters. Choose “Inferred only” to inspect recovered railway paths, or turn off “Inferred rail paths” to hide just those paths. Choose “Dotted only” to inspect the remaining approximate links. Connections with missing or invalid locations are withheld from the map; their source timetable records are retained.</p><p>Filters provides independent Trains, Replacement buses, Affected railways and City labels controls. Each layer can be shown or hidden without changing the others. Only shortcuts change the selected service types; Dotted only changes geometry choices. Timetabled buses retain actual vehicle labels and separate counts. Grey affected railways come from dated notices and do not imply bus frequency or pass eligibility. Geometry, source, pass and fee filters apply to services; closure notices remain independent. Unknown fees never qualify for No mandatory extras. Physical railways without verified service start hidden and have their own switch in Context overlays. Advanced controls contains exact timetable source selection, geometry switches and railway context. Context All/None controls all context layers; Hide all overlays clears services, railway context and stations. Supplemental bus connections without recorded pass eligibility are hidden from pass-scoped views. Controls stay closed initially and can be opened with Filters or dismissed with the close button or Escape. Station dots can follow automatic zoom thresholds, stay on at any zoom, or hide background stops. Wide views space dots out for clarity. Selected stations and the recorded endpoints of their matching routes remain visible; zooming in shows intermediate calls. Shared links retain the selections.</p></div><div class="method-footer"><span id="methodRelease">Dated source snapshot</span><button class="primary-button" id="backToMap">Back to the map <span>↗</span></button></div></dialog>
